  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of what the retrieval surfaced:\n\n**Primary finding \u2014 dead/removed React internal, load-bearing when triggered:**\n`packages/react-scripts/config/webpack.config.js` aliases `scheduler/tracing` \u2192 `scheduler/tracing-profiling` (and `react-dom$` \u2192 `react-dom/profiling`) whenever a production build runs with `--profile` (`isEnvProductionProfile`). This was CRA's bridge to React DevTools' old interaction-tracing profiler API from the React 16/17 era. In React 18.2.0's `packages/scheduler/package.json`, the `files` field only lists `index.js`, `unstable_mock.js`, and `unstable_post_task.js` \u2014 no `tracing.js` or `tracing-profiling.js` exist in the package at all (confirmed both paths 404 on read). `react-dom/profiling` itself is still valid (present in `react-dom/package.json`'s `exports`), so only the `scheduler/tracing*` half is dead. Any `react-scripts build --profile` invocation will fail webpack module resolution \u2014 this is load-bearing dead code, not vestigial, because it's wired into an active, user-facing CLI flag.\n\n**Secondary finding \u2014 vestigial/disabled lint reference:**\n`packages/eslint-config-react-app/index.js` has `'react/no-deprecated': 'warn'` commented out, with a note citing unresolved false-positive issues (#5204). This is CRA's own acknowledgment that the mechanism meant to catch deprecated React API usage in user code is dead in their config \u2014 non-load-bearing (nothing depends on it), but it documents CRA never re-enabled deprecation checking as React's API surface moved on.\n\n**Non-findings worth noting:** CRA's own templates (`cra-template`, `cra-template-typescript`) already use the modern `react-dom/client` `createRoot` API, not legacy `ReactDOM.render`/`ReactDOM.hydrate` \u2014 no dead code there.",
